class Solution {
    public ListNode swapPairs(ListNode head) {
        
        // Base case:
        // If the list is empty OR has only one node,
        // no swapping is needed → return head as it is
        if (head == null || head.next == null) {
            return head;
        }

        // First node of the current pair
        ListNode first = head;

        // Second node of the current pair
        ListNode second = head.next;

        // Recursively call for the rest of the list
        // Example: if list is 1→2→3→4
        // after swapping (1,2), we call swapPairs on 3→4
        first.next = swapPairs(second.next);

        // Perform the actual swap
        // Make second point to first
        second.next = first;

        // Return new head of this pair
        // (second becomes the new head after swap)
        return second;
    }
}

🔁 Dry Run (1 → 2 → 3 → 4)
Initial Call:
swapPairs(1 → 2 → 3 → 4)
Step 1:
first = 1
second = 2

Now recursively call:

first.next = swapPairs(3 → 4)
Step 2 (Recursive Call):
swapPairs(3 → 4)
first = 3
second = 4

Again call:

first.next = swapPairs(null)
Step 3 (Base Case):
swapPairs(null) → return null
Back to Step 2:
3.next = null
4.next = 3

Result:

4 → 3
Back to Step 1:

Now:

1.next = 4 → 3
2.next = 1

Final result:

2 → 1 → 4 → 3
🧠 Call Stack Visualization
swap(1,2)
   ↳ swap(3,4)
       ↳ swap(null)

Then it builds back:

swap(3,4) → 4→3
swap(1,2) → 2→1→4→3
⏱ Complexity
Time Complexity:
O(n)
You visit each node exactly once.
Space Complexity:
O(n) (recursion stack)

Because:

Each recursive call waits on the stack
In worst case (n nodes), stack depth = n/2 ≈ O(n)
